**Key idea:** Express the rotation $A^R$ in terms of the reversal matrix $J$ and the transpose, then show $A = A^R$ forces $A$ to commute with $J$, which decomposes $\mathbb{R}^n$ into a symmetric part (real eigenvalues) and a skew-symmetric part (purely imaginary eigenvalues).

---

**Proof.**

Let $J$ be the $n\times n$ reversal (anti-diagonal) matrix, $J_{ij}=\delta_{i,n-1-j}$. Then $J^T=J$ and $J^2=I$.

**Claim 1.** $A^R = JA^T$.

Indeed, from the definition $(A^R)_{p,q}=A_{q,\,n-1-p}$ (the entry at position $(i,j)$ of $A$ moves to position $(n-1-j,\,i)$ under counter-clockwise rotation). Meanwhile
$$(JA^T)_{p,q}=(A^T)_{n-1-p,\,q}=A_{q,\,n-1-p}=(A^R)_{p,q}.$$
So $A^R=JA^T$.

**Claim 2.** $A^{R^2}=JAJ$.

Applying the formula twice:
$$((A^R)^R)_{p,q}=(A^R)_{q,\,n-1-p}=A_{n-1-p,\,n-1-q}=(JAJ)_{p,q}.$$

**Using $A=A^R$.** From $A=A^R=JA^T$ and $J^2=I$ we get
$$A^T = JA. \tag{$\ast$}$$
Applying $R$ to both sides of $A=A^R$ gives $A^R=A^{R^2}$, hence $A=A^{R^2}=JAJ$, i.e.
$$JA=AJ. \tag{$\dagger$}$$

**Decomposition.** Since $J$ is real symmetric with $J^2=I$, it is diagonalizable with eigenvalues $\pm1$. Let
$$V_+=\{v:Jv=v\},\qquad V_-=\{v:Jv=-v\}.$$
Then $\mathbb{R}^n=V_+\oplus V_-$ (orthogonal direct sum). By $(\dagger)$, $A$ commutes with $J$, so $A$ preserves both $V_+$ and $V_-$: if $v\in V_\pm$ then $J(Av)=A(Jv)=\pm Av$, so $Av\in V_\pm$.

**On $V_+$ the restriction is symmetric.** For $v\in V_+$, using $(\ast)$ and $Av\in V_+$:
$$A^T v = JAv = Av \qquad(\text{since }Av\in V_+).$$
Thus for $u,v\in V_+$, $\langle Au,v\rangle=\langle u,A^Tv\rangle=\langle u,Av\rangle$, so $A|_{V_+}$ is self-adjoint. A real self-adjoint operator has **real** eigenvalues.

**On $V_-$ the restriction is skew-symmetric.** For $v\in V_-$, using $(\ast)$ and $Av\in V_-$:
$$A^T v = JAv = -Av \qquad(\text{since }Av\in V_-).$$
Thus for $u,v\in V_-$, $\langle Au,v\rangle=\langle u,A^Tv\rangle=\langle u,-Av\rangle=-\langle u,Av\rangle$, so $A|_{V_-}$ is skew-adjoint. A real skew-adjoint operator has **purely imaginary** eigenvalues (if $Aw=\mu w$ over $\mathbb{C}$, then $\mu\langle w,w\rangle=\langle Aw,w\rangle=\langle w,A^*w\rangle=\langle w,-Aw\rangle=-\bar\mu\langle w,w\rangle$, giving $\mu=-\bar\mu$).

**Conclusion.** Every eigenvalue of $A$ is an eigenvalue of either $A|_{V_+}$ (hence real, $\operatorname{Im}\lambda=0$) or of $A|_{V_-}$ (hence purely imaginary, $\operatorname{Re}\lambda=0$). Therefore for every eigenvalue $\lambda$ of $A$,
$$\operatorname{Re}\lambda=0\quad\text{or}\quad \operatorname{Im}\lambda=0. \qquad\blacksquare$$

